{"results":[{"url":"https://www.thecanadianpressnews.ca/fact_checking/fox-news-isnt-banned-in-canada-despite-viral-posts-claiming-otherwise-but-it-could-be/article_3566ffe6-45a5-54bf-981a-51f72ba0eada.html","final_url":"https://www.thecanadianpressnews.ca/fact_checking/fox-news-isnt-banned-in-canada-despite-viral-posts-claiming-otherwise-but-it-could-be/article_3566ffe6-45a5-54bf-981a-51f72ba0eada.html","title":"Fox News isn't banned in Canada, despite viral posts claiming otherwise – but it could be","description":"Viral posts celebrating the end of Fox News in Canada appeared on social media in June. Claims Canada blocked Fox News from its airwaves are false. Canada's broadcast regulator says","language":"en","text":"Viral posts celebrating the end of Fox News in Canada appeared on social media in June. Claims Canada blocked Fox News from its airwaves are false. Canada's broadcast regulator says Fox News is still available countrywide and similar claims about the channel's removal have been around since at least 2012. However, in 2023 a Canadian LGBTQ rights group filed an application to have Fox News removed from the list of non-Canadian stations allowed to air in Canada. The consultation process about its removal is still ongoing.\n\n### THE CLAIM\n\n\"BREAKING: Fox News is blocked in Canada. Now, number 47 is a nobody … well done Canada,\" reads a June Threads post with more than 100,000 likes.\n\n### RATING: False\n\nThe Fox News Channel is an American conservative television network and website.\n\nThe Canadian Radio-television and Telecommunications Commission (CRTC) approved Fox News on Canada's digital airwaves in 2004.\n\nIn an email, the CRTC said Fox News is still available in Canada and the channel appears on a CRTC list of television stations allowed to broadcast in the country.\n\nHowever, there is a pathway for Canada to block the sometimes controversial channel that consistently tops the list of the most viewed cable news network in the United States, earning 66 per cent of the prime time cable news audience in the first quarter of 2025.\n\nIn 2023, LGBTQ rights organization Egale Canada submitted an application to the CRTC to remove Fox News from the list of non-Canadian programming services and stations allowed in Canada.\n\nIn its open letter to the CRTC, Egale Canada said former Fox News host Tucker Carlson made false and offensive claims about LGBTQ people on his program. The organization argued his comments violated Canadian broadcasting standards for content exposing a group to hate.\n\nThe CRTC received more than 7,000 submissions after calls for public consultation about banning Fox News in Canada.\n\nIn a September 2023 response to Egale Canada, then-CRTC secretary-general Claude Doucet said Canada's regulations for non-Canadian television services needed a review, essentially delaying a decision about blocking Fox News until the review's completion.\n\n\"A public consultation on the regulatory approach for overseeing non-Canadian television services will start in the coming months,\" Doucet wrote.\n\nIn an email to The Canadian Press, the CRTC said it \"intends\" to launch a consultation.\n\n\"As this matter is currently before the CRTC, it would not be appropriate for the CRTC to comment further,\" a spokesperson wrote.\n\nFor its part, Fox News argued Egale Canada's application was \"moot,\" because the subject of its complaint, Tucker Carlson, was fired in April 2023 and blocking Fox News would be a \"grossly disproportionate and unprecedented\" response and contrary to the Canadian Charter of Rights and Freedoms.\n\nThere is precedent for banning non-Canadian networks in Canada.\n\nThe CRTC removed the Russian state-controlled TV channels RT and RT France from its list of approved non-Canadian programming services after Russia's invasion of Ukraine in 2022.\n\nIn its decision, the CRTC said it was \"concerned with programming from a foreign country that seeks to undermine the sovereignty of another country, demean Canadians of a particular ethnic background and undermine democratic institutions within Canada.\"\n\nFox News operates a website as well as a cable network. The Fox News website is accessible in Canada, with the exception of Facebook and Instagram due to Meta's news ban, in place since 2023.\n\nHowever, Threads, the Twitter-like social media platform that's also a Meta product, does allow content from Fox News to be shared.\n\n### SOURCES\n\nClaims posted to Threads June 17, 2025 here (archived) and here (archived) and Facebook June 17, 2025 (archived)\n\nRevised list of non-Canadian programming services and stations authorized for distribution. CRTC website. Accessed June 24, 2025 (archived)\n\nDavid Mikkelson. Was the Fox News Channel Banned in Canada? Snopes website. Oct. 18, 2012 (archived)\n\nAli Swenson. Fox News is not banned in Canada or the UK. The Associated Press website. Dec. 14, 2021 (archived)\n\nDavid Bauder, Randall Chase and Geoff Mulvihill. Fox, Dominion reach $787M settlement over election claims. The Associated Press website. April 18, 2023 (archived)\n\nMark Mwachiro. Here's the 1st Quarter of 2025 Cable News Ratings. Adweek website. April 2, 2025 (archived)\n\nOpen Letter: Egale Canada calls on the CRTC to Hold a Public Consultation on the Broadcasting of the American Fox News Channel in Canada. Egale Canada website. April 4, 2023 (archived)\n\nBroadcasting - Secretary General Letter addressed to Helen Kennedy (Egale Canada). CRTC website. Sept. 19, 2023 (archived)\n\nMartin Pengelly. Tucker Carlson claims in book Fox News firing was part of $787.5m settlement. The Guardian website. July 26, 2023 (archived)\n\nRT and RT France can no longer be distributed by Canadian television service providers. CRTC website. March 16, 2022 (archived)\n\nJessica Mundie. Canadians will no longer have access to news content on Facebook and Instagram, Meta says. CBC News website. June 22, 2023 (archived)\n\n### ABOUT CANADIAN PRESS FACT CHECKS\n\nYou can find out more about The Canadian Press here and about Canadian Press Fact Checks here. To reach our fact-checking team with any tips, corrections or comments, please email us at cpfactcheck@thecanadianpress.com.","author":"Marissa Birnie","published_date":"2025-06-24T16:11:00-04:00","latency_ms":2768.1359680136666,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}